INT. DISUSED CURLING RINK - NIGHT

Half the lights are dead. A sheet of ice gone gray, scarred by years of rental hockey. Folding chairs along the boards. A cooler. A speaker on a bucket playing nothing.

Six people wait for a seventh who is late.

COACH HALVORSEN, 50s, whistle on a shoelace, sits on the penalty box bench and pares an apple with a pocketknife. DESHAWN "DEE" OKORO, 30s, goalie pads already strapped, stares at his own skates. NADIA PETROVIC, late 20s, captain's C stitched crooked on her jersey, tapes a stick that is already taped. BIG TOMÁS REYES, 40s, enormous, eats sunflower seeds one at a time. WINNIE CHO, 19, new, sits straighter than the others. And LUCAS BRANDT, 30s, sponsor's nephew, wears a pressed team jacket and nothing else that says he plays.

Nobody speaks. Tomás spits a shell into a cup.

COACH HALVORSEN
Ice time ends at ten.

NADIA
It's nine-forty.

COACH HALVORSEN
So.

Nadia stands, skates onto the ice, and does one lap alone. Winnie watches her. Lucas watches Winnie.

LUCAS
Is she always like this?

Tomás turns his head slowly.

BIG TOMÁS
Like what.

LUCAS
Intense.

Tomás goes back to his seeds.

DEE
(not looking up)
You're in Winnie's spot.

LUCAS
It's a bench.

DEE
She sits there.

Lucas does not move. Winnie does not ask him to. She takes her helmet off the floor and puts it on, chin strap clicked slowly.

COACH HALVORSEN
Dee. You're starting.

DEE
I'm starting every game.

COACH HALVORSEN
I know.

He folds the knife. Eats the apple core and all.

Nadia finishes her lap and stops at the boards in front of Lucas, close enough that her breath fogs his jacket.

NADIA
Who gave you a roster spot?

LUCAS
My uncle owns the sponsor.

NADIA
Right. And who gave him a tax break?

LUCAS
I didn't come here to fight.

NADIA
You didn't come here to play either.

WINNIE
Leave it.

NADIA
He took your minutes.

WINNIE
I said leave it.

Silence. The speaker crackles and cuts out again.

Tomás stands. He is very tall standing. He picks up Lucas's stick from the rack, studies it, and hands it back blade first.

BIG TOMÁS
Tape's wrong.

LUCAS
I taped it this morning.

BIG TOMÁS
That's what I said.

Lucas takes the stick. His hand shakes a little on the shaft and he hides it by gripping tighter.

Coach Halvorsen blows his whistle once. Everyone stops.

COACH HALVORSEN
Lucas. Line one.

NADIA
Coach.

COACH HALVORSEN
Line one, with Winnie and Tomás.

NADIA
That's my line.

COACH HALVORSEN
Then you'll be on the bench.

Nadia looks at him. At Lucas. At Winnie. She takes the C off her chest in one hard tear and drops it on the ice, where it sits, a small crooked patch.

Nobody moves to pick it up.

DEE
(low)
Nadia.

NADIA
Don't.

She skates off to the far end. Slams the boards with her stick. The sound goes up into the dead lights and comes back.

Winnie steps onto the ice. Lucas follows, wobbles, catches himself on her sleeve. She lets him hold it for a second, then removes his hand.

WINNIE
Skate wide. Don't touch anyone.

LUCAS
Why not?

WINNIE
Because she's waiting for you to.

At the far end Nadia has stopped. She turns. Her stick comes up.

TOMÁS
(under his breath)
Here we go.

Dee pulls his mask down. The puck drops by itself, dropped from Coach's fist onto the ice with a click, and then there is only the sound of blades.

Nadia is already moving. Lucas tries to turn and cannot. She hits him hip-first into the boards and the whole rink shakes. He goes down. She stands over him, breathing, her stick lifted to the height of her shoulder.

Tomás is on her in two strides, wrapping her arms. Winnie is between them. Dee has left the net and is standing at the blue line, mask pushed up, pads dripping frost.

NADIA
(through teeth)
Let go of me.

BIG TOMÁS
When you drop it.

She doesn't. He doesn't. The stick shakes between them.

COACH HALVORSEN
(from the bench, calm)
Ten o'clock.

The lights above the far end click off, one bank after another, until the only light left is over the six of them, and then that one goes too.
